Each declaration style fits a different situation, and the snippet below also proves the two facts interviewers probe: Java zero-fills automatically, and `length` is a field. Run it as a plain Java 17 console app.

```java
public class ArrayDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Style 1: declare size, fill later
        int[] scores = new int[5];
        System.out.println(scores[0]); // 0  — Java zero-fills automatically

        scores[0] = 95;
        scores[1] = 72;
        // scores[2], [3], [4] remain 0

        // Style 2: declare and initialize with values in one line
        String[] days = {"Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri"};
        System.out.println(days[0]); // Mon

        // Style 3: new keyword with explicit values (useful when size is computed)
        double[] prices = new double[]{9.99, 14.50, 3.75};

        // Length is a field — no parentheses
        System.out.println(scores.length); // 5
        // System.out.println(scores.length()); // COMPILE ERROR — common mistake

        // Default for object arrays is null, not empty string
        String[] names = new String[3];
        System.out.println(names[0]); // null
    }
}
```

Notice the two traps in one place: `scores.length()` would refuse to compile, and `names[0]` is `null` rather than an empty string — object arrays default to `null`, not to a "blank" value.

The snippet below puts the bug and three safe alternatives side by side so the difference is concrete. Drop it into a Java 17 console app — the first loop crashes, the rest don't.

```java
public class BoundsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] scores = {90, 85, 78, 92, 88}; // 5 elements: indices 0 to 4

        // WRONG — i <= scores.length means i goes 0,1,2,3,4,5
        // When i=5: scores[5] does not exist — exception thrown
        for (int i = 0; i <= scores.length; i++) {  // BUG: <= should be <
            System.out.println(scores[i]);
        }

        // CORRECT — i < scores.length means i stops at 4
        for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(scores[i]); // safe: 90, 85, 78, 92, 88
        }

        // SAFEST for read-only — enhanced for loop, no index needed
        for (int score : scores) {
            System.out.println(score); // compiler guarantees no out-of-bounds
        }

        // Direct access — always check before using a variable index
        int index = 10;
        if (index >= 0 && index < scores.length) {
            System.out.println(scores[index]); // safe
        }
    }
}
```

The takeaway: the strict `<` loop and the enhanced for loop are both safe, but the enhanced for is the one you literally cannot get wrong, because there is no index to manage.

Watching the same job done both ways makes the trade-off tangible: the array hits a wall at its fixed size, while the `ArrayList` just keeps accepting elements. Run it as a Java 17 console app.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ArrayVsArrayList {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // ARRAY — fixed size, direct primitive storage
        int[] topScores = new int[3];
        topScores[0] = 95;
        topScores[1] = 88;
        topScores[2] = 76;
        // topScores[3] = 70;  // COMPILE ERROR — index 3 does not exist
        System.out.println(topScores.length); // 3

        // ARRAYLIST — dynamic, grows as needed
        ArrayList<Integer> playerScores = new ArrayList<>();
        playerScores.add(95);
        playerScores.add(88);
        playerScores.add(76);
        playerScores.add(70);  // no problem — ArrayList resizes automatically
        playerScores.remove(0); // removes the first element (95)
        System.out.println(playerScores.size()); // 3
        System.out.println(playerScores.get(0)); // 88
    }
}
```

The array refuses the fourth element at compile time; the `ArrayList` absorbs it and even lets you remove from the middle — that flexibility is the whole reason it exists, and the only price is the boxing of each `int` into an `Integer`.

These two methods look almost identical but behave completely differently, and running them is the fastest way to internalise the rule. Try it as a Java 17 console app and watch the two print statements.

```java
public class ArrayPassDemo {

    // Modifies elements — the change IS visible to the caller
    static void doubleAll(int[] arr) {
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            arr[i] = arr[i] * 2; // reaches through the copied address to shared heap
        }
    }

    // Reassigns the parameter — the caller's variable is NOT affected
    static void tryReplace(int[] arr) {
        arr = new int[]{1, 2, 3}; // only changes the local copy of the reference
        // the caller's original array is completely untouched
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] scores = {90, 85, 78};

        doubleAll(scores);
        System.out.println(scores[0]); // 180 — change visible, same heap object

        tryReplace(scores);
        System.out.println(scores[0]); // 180 — unchanged, reassignment did not escape
    }
}
```

The contrast is the whole answer: `doubleAll` reaches through the shared address and the change sticks, while `tryReplace` only rebinds its local copy of the address, so the caller's `scores` never moves.
